{
  "step_1": "The decision reason focuses on the least probable outcomes of each lottery, specifically the most favorable outcome with the lowest probability. In this context, 'most favorable' refers to the least negative outcome (i.e., the one involving a smaller loss or no loss) among the outcomes with the minimum probability. For Lottery A, the least probable outcome is -3000 Euros with 0.2% probability. For Lottery B, the least probable outcome is -6000 Euros with 0.1% probability. The minimum probability in A is 0.2%, and in B it is 0.1%. The reason compares the outcomes associated with these minimum probabilities. Since the reason prefers the lottery with the more favorable (i.e., less negative) least probable outcome, it would prefer Lottery A over B if comparing -3000 (A) to -6000 (B), even though the probabilities differ. Thus, the decision reason can be applied to this decision problem, and it would lead to a preference for Lottery A.",
